    {
      "id": "e9373845-8911-4ea7-80d4-82658d849fc2",
      "sentence": "Athenian democracy had three standing contradictions: Athens was a slave society, it denied women any political or legal identity, and it ran an empire it held by massacres.",
      "blurb": "Slavery and the exclusion of women were normal across the ancient world, so these are contradictions from a modern point of view. Slave labour also gave many citizens the free time to attend the Assembly and hold office.",
      "quote": {
        "text": "…they were also a people who considered women, slaves, and non-Greeks to be biologically and intellectually inferior, who committed brutal acts of barbarism against non-combatants in time of war and selfishly exploited their fellow Greeks in the furtherance of their aggressive imperialistic aims.",
        "timestamp": "00:17:02"
      },
      "transcriptRef": "transcripts/01-why-athenian-democracy-matters.md [00:14:01]",
